much for visiting us virtually.

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:09.290
So Scott Williamson is a
political scientist and he's in

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:14.630
the Department of Social
Political Sciences at Bocconi.

00:00:14.630 --> 00:00:19.440
And today we'll hear
about people's perceptions

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:25.620
and attitudes towards migrants
and, you know, Scott, so,

00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:26.920
you know, you're,

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:29.170
we discussed you're in a
interdisciplinary audience

00:00:29.170 --> 00:00:31.850
and this seminar is generally
very interdisciplinary,

00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:33.710
so last week we had an
anthropology speaking,

00:00:33.710 --> 00:00:36.083
the previous week, an economist speaking,

00:00:36.083 --> 00:00:39.590
and so we tend to get
participation from people

00:00:39.590 --> 00:00:42.540
in different disciplines
and, you know, and sometimes,

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:44.140
you know, of course disciplinary

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:49.950
like cultures vary and
sometimes, you know, people might

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:54.320
just unmute themselves and ask a question

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:57.390
if there's something like
clarifying, they wanna...

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:01.170
All right, thank you, please take it away.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:06.720
Oh, and Teresa also
wanted me to announce that

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:09.550
we're now doing a survey on the seminars

00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:10.900
so she'll put the link in the chat

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:14.127
so if the participants can just click

00:01:14.127 --> 00:01:16.801
and provide us your feedback.

00:01:16.801 --> 00:01:18.043
That'd be great, thank you.

00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:21.010
- Great, thanks so much.

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:22.760
Thank you for those of you who are here.

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:23.940
Thank you to the program

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:26.250
for the opportunity to share my work.

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:28.710
The title of the project
is Combining Perspective-

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:30.630
Getting with Information Delivery

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:33.003
to Increase Public Support for Refugees.

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:35.910
This is joint work with a team of scholars

00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:38.990
who I've worked with
previously on related projects,

00:01:38.990 --> 00:01:42.050
Claire Adida and Lauren Prather at UCSD,

00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:44.980
Adeline Lo at University
of Wisconsin-Madison,

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:49.310
and Melina Platas at New
York University Abu Dhabi.

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:52.870
So I'm very lucky to be working with them

00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:56.030
and we all look forward to
any feedback you might have.

00:01:56.030 --> 00:01:58.780
And I'm completely happy to
take questions throughout,

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:02.540
so please feel free to
interrupt me at any point

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:04.260
if something's unclear or

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:07.163
if you wanna ask a clarifying question.

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:13.070
Let's see, okay, so I
wanna start by just noting

00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:15.730
the extent to which for
the past several decades,

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:18.360
or really since we have
public opinion data,

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:20.570
the American public has generally been

00:02:20.570 --> 00:02:22.700
opposed to welcoming refugees

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:25.240
or specific policies designed to bring in

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:27.360
specific refugees from specific conflicts,

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:29.570
even those in which the United States

00:02:29.570 --> 00:02:31.380
has been very much involved.

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:33.790
So we have evidence on
this from World War II

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:36.740
with Jewish refugees from Nazi Germany,

00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:39.810
but then also from Hungary in 1958

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:44.150
following the Vietnam
War in the late 1970s,

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:47.490
with Cuba in 1980 and then less so but

00:02:47.490 --> 00:02:49.700
in 1990s with ethnic Albanians.

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:52.540
But the general pattern
here is one in which

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:55.850
opposition two refugees
has historically been

00:02:55.850 --> 00:02:56.863
relatively high.

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:00.850
And we've seen this
very much continue into

00:03:00.850 --> 00:03:04.950
more recent conflicts,
most notably with Syria,

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:09.400
so from polling by Gallup in 2015

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:12.010
at the height of the
Syrian refugee crisis,

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:14.230
37% of respondents

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:16.650
approved of welcoming
in more Syrian refugees,

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:19.710
but a fairly large majority disapproved.

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:24.093
So this is a consistent pattern
that we see over and over.

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:26.380
This did change to some extent

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:27.960
following the election of President Trump

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:32.080
when actually Americans over
those four years became more

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:33.700
willing to endorse immigration

00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:36.300
and the admittance of
refugees specifically.

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:37.300
But what we did see,

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:39.750
which wasn't really as
pronounced in recent years

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:42.470
was a growing partisan
gap between Republicans

00:03:42.470 --> 00:03:44.320
and Democrats, so over this period,

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:48.010
Democrats became more
supportive and Republicans

00:03:48.010 --> 00:03:51.233
tended to become more opposed
to admitting refugees.

00:03:52.330 --> 00:03:55.560
And these attitudes are
very much reflected in

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:58.740
the politics and policies
surrounding this issue,

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:02.120
so specifically with Republicans
becoming more opposed,

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:05.150
we saw around this time
as I'm sure you're aware

00:04:05.150 --> 00:04:08.580
that Republican governors
were resisting efforts to

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:10.950
settle Syrians in their states,

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:14.380
and of course, infamously
Donald Trump's Muslim ban

00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:18.970
was proposed in 2015 during
the presidential campaign ban

00:04:18.970 --> 00:04:20.770
following the San Bernardino attacks,

00:04:20.770 --> 00:04:25.560
but also linked to these
arguments about whether or not to

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:27.440
admit refugees from Syria.

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:32.000
So we do see that this
public opinion seems to drive

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:35.363
to some extent policy on this issue.

00:04:37.450 --> 00:04:39.010
And what's also quite common

00:04:39.010 --> 00:04:41.670
is to see that when
politicians are justifying

00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:43.140
these sorts of policies,

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:46.070
there's a lot of misinformation
that gets trafficked

00:04:46.070 --> 00:04:48.970
in attempting to persuade Americans to

00:04:48.970 --> 00:04:52.060
be more restrictive in terms
of immigration or refugees.

00:04:52.060 --> 00:04:55.430
So again, Trump's executive order

00:04:55.430 --> 00:04:58.180
or Muslim ban was
implemented in some fashion.

00:04:58.180 --> 00:05:01.500
And the way this was justified
was to talk about how,

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:04.380
in part how refugees or
those coming to the U.S

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:07.240
often don't get vetted very much at all,

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:08.830
and so who knows who's coming in,

00:05:08.830 --> 00:05:10.610
who knows what kinds of
threats they might pose,

00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:13.590
and of course this is
completely false in terms of how

00:05:13.590 --> 00:05:15.980
the U.S goes about vetting refugees,

00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:19.480
especially but this was a
cornerstone of the policy.

00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:21.610
And even recently,

00:05:21.610 --> 00:05:25.710
we saw this with the 2021

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:28.250
refugee wave of Afghans
coming to the United States

00:05:28.250 --> 00:05:30.230
following the fall of Kabul

00:05:30.230 --> 00:05:32.260
when former president
Trump at the time claimed

00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:35.060
that there is no vetting
of refugees coming to

00:05:35.060 --> 00:05:36.290
America from Afghanistan.

00:05:36.290 --> 00:05:38.590
And again, this was very much false.

00:05:38.590 --> 00:05:40.200
In this specific case,

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:42.010
it doesn't seem to have
been very successful

00:05:42.010 --> 00:05:45.720
because this was a period
in which Americans were more

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:48.803
willing to support admitting Afghans.

00:05:50.050 --> 00:05:53.250
So with all this prevalence
of misinformation

00:05:53.250 --> 00:05:58.150
often used to justify and
promote anti refugee sentiment,

00:05:58.150 --> 00:06:02.400
one common strategy
that seems common sense

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:06.670
for advocates and activists
is to publish information

00:06:06.670 --> 00:06:09.400
trying to correct these misconceptions

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:11.800
in a very factual way.

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:15.530
So this is an example of one such article

00:06:15.530 --> 00:06:18.510
from Human Rights First in 2016,

00:06:18.510 --> 00:06:19.870
where they say top three myths

00:06:19.870 --> 00:06:22.900
about the U.S refugee
resettlement system dispelled,

00:06:22.900 --> 00:06:26.220
and then they go through those
in quite a lot of detail.

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:28.310
And so in this project,

00:06:28.310 --> 00:06:31.530
the primary motivating
question is whether,

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:33.470
and in what context the provision

00:06:33.470 --> 00:06:35.900
of this sort of correcting information

00:06:35.900 --> 00:06:38.140
can actually change
recipients misconceptions.

00:06:38.140 --> 00:06:40.230
And so the first order question here is

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:42.550
when you provide this sort
of factual information,

00:06:42.550 --> 00:06:44.550
do you actually see individuals

00:06:44.550 --> 00:06:49.550
update and correct their false views.

00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:52.660
But then second and
perhaps more importantly,

00:06:52.660 --> 00:06:56.040
we're interested in whether
this correction of information

00:06:56.040 --> 00:06:58.010
has broader spillover benefits

00:06:58.010 --> 00:07:00.430
to reducing prejudice
and negative attitudes

00:07:00.430 --> 00:07:04.070
towards out-groups, in this
case refugees specifically.

00:07:04.070 --> 00:07:07.040
So you might think that when
you correct this misconception

00:07:07.040 --> 00:07:11.200
about vetting and the
extent to which refugees are

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:13.260
looked at very closely
by the U.S government

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:15.330
that people might become less worried

00:07:15.330 --> 00:07:17.490
about security concerns
and that this would

00:07:17.490 --> 00:07:20.670
then spill over into their
hostility towards refugees,

00:07:20.670 --> 00:07:23.990
perhaps their preferences
even on refugee policies

00:07:23.990 --> 00:07:27.980
and maybe even their willingness
to take action on behalf

00:07:27.980 --> 00:07:30.193
of refugees coming to the United States.

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:34.130
And then finally providing
information in this way

00:07:34.130 --> 00:07:37.410
is only one strategy
that advocates have used

00:07:37.410 --> 00:07:39.490
and that scholars have studied in terms of

00:07:39.490 --> 00:07:42.250
trying to reduce prejudice
towards out-groups.

00:07:42.250 --> 00:07:46.070
And one other common basket of strategies

00:07:46.070 --> 00:07:47.930
ties to leverage empathy

00:07:47.930 --> 00:07:50.440
specifically to generate
empathy for out-groups

00:07:50.440 --> 00:07:52.120
and reduce prejudice in that way.

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:53.860
And so we're also interested in comparing

00:07:53.860 --> 00:07:57.730
the relative effectiveness of
information provision relative

00:07:57.730 --> 00:08:00.293
to these empathy strategies.

00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:05.190
So talking a little more about

00:08:05.190 --> 00:08:10.190
why information might fail to or it might

00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:14.180
what the literature says about
this information correction

00:08:14.180 --> 00:08:15.170
in general,

00:08:15.170 --> 00:08:17.680
moving away from prejudice
reduction for a minute,

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:20.990
we do find that people tend to update

00:08:20.990 --> 00:08:24.700
falsely held beliefs when it is corrected,

00:08:24.700 --> 00:08:26.390
but when this starts to get political

00:08:26.390 --> 00:08:30.000
or is tied into issues of controversy,

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:32.320
that becomes less the case.

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:34.670
And one area in which that is true

00:08:34.670 --> 00:08:36.410
is with prejudice reduction.

00:08:36.410 --> 00:08:38.770
And we have research on this question

00:08:38.770 --> 00:08:40.280
about immigrants and refugees,

00:08:40.280 --> 00:08:42.580
specifically finding mixed results

00:08:42.580 --> 00:08:45.159
in line with that broader pattern.

00:08:45.159 --> 00:08:46.960
So Facchini et al for instance do show

00:08:46.960 --> 00:08:51.230
large positive effects
of information about how

00:08:51.230 --> 00:08:55.080
immigrants would benefit
Japan's aging workforce

00:08:55.080 --> 00:08:57.080
on Japanese citizens,

00:08:57.080 --> 00:09:00.980
attitudes and support
towards more immigration.

00:09:00.980 --> 00:09:03.240
But that tends to be a bit of an outlier,

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:06.200
other studies, for instance,
Adida et al show that

00:09:06.200 --> 00:09:07.840
when you tell Americans how little

00:09:07.840 --> 00:09:09.400
relatively speaking their country

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:11.810
did to welcome Syrian refugees,

00:09:11.810 --> 00:09:14.360
this doesn't actually
convince them that the U.S

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:16.410
should be doing more,

00:09:16.410 --> 00:09:21.250
likewise Sides and Citrin show that the,

00:09:21.250 --> 00:09:24.000
when you correct people's misperceptions

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:26.980
about how many immigrants
there are in the United States,

00:09:26.980 --> 00:09:29.950
which we might think would be linked to

00:09:29.950 --> 00:09:32.500
how threatening they
perceive immigrants to be

00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:35.790
that this doesn't actually
have much spillover into

00:09:35.790 --> 00:09:38.140
how they view immigrants
or immigration policy.

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:41.500
So there are three reasons

00:09:41.500 --> 00:09:43.410
in thinking through these mixed findings

00:09:43.410 --> 00:09:46.300
for why this information provision may

00:09:46.300 --> 00:09:48.860
fail to shift attitudes because of course

00:09:48.860 --> 00:09:50.750
there are all sorts of
different information,

00:09:50.750 --> 00:09:54.320
pieces of information that
you can provide people with.

00:09:54.320 --> 00:09:56.160
And so the first may be that

00:09:56.160 --> 00:09:58.230
the facts are just already
known to individuals,

00:09:58.230 --> 00:10:00.410
maybe they won't even admit that,

00:10:00.410 --> 00:10:02.290
but they already know what
you're trying to correct

00:10:02.290 --> 00:10:04.233
and so it's just not that helpful.

00:10:06.040 --> 00:10:08.690
The second is that the facts are new,

00:10:08.690 --> 00:10:11.720
you are changing the way
they think about something,

00:10:11.720 --> 00:10:15.230
but it's not actually relevant
to how they form attitudes

00:10:15.230 --> 00:10:17.140
towards an out-group, for instance,

00:10:17.140 --> 00:10:20.330
or the behaviors they might
take towards that group as well.

00:10:20.330 --> 00:10:22.820
So if it is one of these two reasons

00:10:22.820 --> 00:10:26.160
that generally explains the
null effects I just mentioned,

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:29.540
then finding the kind
of correct information

00:10:29.540 --> 00:10:32.470
in the right context would
be an effective strategy

00:10:32.470 --> 00:10:37.250
to perhaps increase
favorability towards refugees.

00:10:37.250 --> 00:10:40.290
But of course there is a third possibility

00:10:40.290 --> 00:10:43.080
for which we have a lot of
evidence in other contexts

00:10:43.080 --> 00:10:46.380
which is that maybe the facts
are new and they are relevant,

00:10:46.380 --> 00:10:47.730
but individuals biases

00:10:47.730 --> 00:10:50.160
in terms of how they process information

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:51.460
causes them to resist

00:10:51.460 --> 00:10:53.800
actually believing this new information

00:10:53.800 --> 00:10:57.000
or to incorporate it into
how they're thinking about

00:10:57.000 --> 00:10:59.300
these groups and if this
is generally widespread,

00:10:59.300 --> 00:11:02.810
then we might not find this strategy

00:11:02.810 --> 00:11:04.823
to be particularly effective.

00:11:07.070 --> 00:11:10.340
There's another possible problem here

00:11:10.340 --> 00:11:12.000
that we wanted to flag,

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:15.090
which is that correcting information

00:11:15.090 --> 00:11:16.740
in some cases may be counterproductive

00:11:16.740 --> 00:11:18.720
if it ends up finding threats

00:11:18.720 --> 00:11:20.650
and getting people to think about concerns

00:11:20.650 --> 00:11:23.260
they wouldn't otherwise
have thought about.

00:11:23.260 --> 00:11:26.979
So if we think about
informational interventions

00:11:26.979 --> 00:11:29.130
that are often used by advocates

00:11:29.130 --> 00:11:32.360
or politicians talking about refugees,

00:11:32.360 --> 00:11:34.710
they often cite the economic contributions

00:11:34.710 --> 00:11:37.970
that refugees make to the
United States, for instance,

00:11:37.970 --> 00:11:41.210
or the fact that there
is very low security risk

00:11:41.210 --> 00:11:43.240
from bringing refugees in.

00:11:43.240 --> 00:11:44.120
But in doing this,

00:11:44.120 --> 00:11:47.360
you may actually inadvertently
raise the salient

00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:50.100
of potential economic
and security concerns

00:11:50.100 --> 00:11:52.640
or perhaps people will think that

00:11:52.640 --> 00:11:56.140
others are concerned about
this maybe I should be too.

00:11:56.140 --> 00:11:58.440
So I don't wanna pick on
anyone specifically here,

00:11:58.440 --> 00:12:01.000
but I think this infographic on the slide

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:02.530
is a pretty good example of

00:12:02.530 --> 00:12:05.320
something that could
have this sort of effect,

00:12:05.320 --> 00:12:08.760
so here they say, refugees
are economic contributors,

00:12:08.760 --> 00:12:11.550
clearly trying to address
the potential concern

00:12:11.550 --> 00:12:14.370
someone might have that
refugees come to the country

00:12:14.370 --> 00:12:18.800
and then subsist on
government support, et cetera,

00:12:18.800 --> 00:12:20.080
don't actually help the economy.

00:12:20.080 --> 00:12:21.750
And so they say the top three industries

00:12:21.750 --> 00:12:23.700
that employ refugees are

00:12:23.700 --> 00:12:27.230
manufacturing, the health
sector, and general services.

00:12:27.230 --> 00:12:29.970
But in looking at this it's
possible, we don't know,

00:12:29.970 --> 00:12:32.210
but it's possible that
someone would be well,

00:12:32.210 --> 00:12:34.250
okay, maybe refugees are then competing

00:12:34.250 --> 00:12:37.730
with Americans for jobs in
these very important industries

00:12:37.730 --> 00:12:42.120
and so you've actually primed
an economic threat concern

00:12:42.120 --> 00:12:43.970
that ends up being counterproductive.

00:12:45.850 --> 00:12:49.770
So setting this information
provision aside for a second,

00:12:49.770 --> 00:12:52.130
another set of strategies as I mentioned

00:12:52.130 --> 00:12:54.530
for trying to reduce prejudice

00:12:54.530 --> 00:12:57.340
or increase inclusive
attitudes and behaviors

00:12:57.340 --> 00:12:59.320
is to leverage emotion

00:12:59.320 --> 00:13:03.270
and specifically empathy towards
a member of the out-group,

00:13:03.270 --> 00:13:05.930
to then have that broaden into

00:13:05.930 --> 00:13:08.390
more inclusive attitudes more generally.

00:13:08.390 --> 00:13:10.280
So there's a large body of research

00:13:10.280 --> 00:13:12.340
showing that this tends
to be quite effective

00:13:12.340 --> 00:13:15.830
including with regards to
attitudes towards refugees

00:13:15.830 --> 00:13:18.120
and immigrants specifically.

00:13:18.120 --> 00:13:19.970
So again, Adida et al show that

00:13:19.970 --> 00:13:23.410
if you encourage people
to take the perspective

00:13:23.410 --> 00:13:25.590
of a refugee fleeing from Syria,

00:13:25.590 --> 00:13:27.390
that this might increase their willingness

00:13:27.390 --> 00:13:29.280
to write a letter to the White House

00:13:29.280 --> 00:13:32.440
advocating for lifting the refugee cap.

00:13:32.440 --> 00:13:35.970
Likewise Broockman and Kalla
show that if you give people

00:13:35.970 --> 00:13:38.130
the perspective of an
unauthorized immigrant,

00:13:38.130 --> 00:13:41.650
show them what it's like
to be in that position,

00:13:41.650 --> 00:13:43.990
that this can reduce negative attitudes

00:13:43.990 --> 00:13:45.990
towards this group as well

00:13:45.990 --> 00:13:50.290
in part by creating more
empathy for their situation,

00:13:50.290 --> 00:13:51.970
and then work that I've done with

00:13:51.970 --> 00:13:54.680
these same co-authors
as well as some others

00:13:54.680 --> 00:13:57.870
shows that priming people on
their own family's histories

00:13:57.870 --> 00:14:00.620
with migration can also trigger

00:14:00.620 --> 00:14:02.720
empathy towards migrants today,

00:14:02.720 --> 00:14:06.610
and perhaps then shape the
way they view immigrants

00:14:06.610 --> 00:14:08.703
and immigration policy.

00:14:09.610 --> 00:14:14.100
So we in this study wanted
to build on this work

00:14:14.100 --> 00:14:15.490
with two questions

00:14:15.490 --> 00:14:17.570
and I'll talk a little more
about these in a minute,

00:14:17.570 --> 00:14:19.560
but the first was whether there might be

00:14:19.560 --> 00:14:22.740
beneficial interactions
between providing information

00:14:22.740 --> 00:14:24.710
to correct a misconception

00:14:24.710 --> 00:14:27.610
and also generating empathy in the same

00:14:27.610 --> 00:14:30.010
sort of prejudice reduction intervention.

00:14:30.010 --> 00:14:32.300
So if we use these in tandem,

00:14:32.300 --> 00:14:36.140
does that actually create better results?

00:14:36.140 --> 00:14:38.250
And then the second is thinking about

00:14:38.250 --> 00:14:40.610
how these empathy generating interventions

00:14:40.610 --> 00:14:43.220
may work more or less effectively

00:14:43.220 --> 00:14:45.090
with certain types of people

00:14:45.090 --> 00:14:48.280
and specifically whether
individuals who just

00:14:48.280 --> 00:14:50.950
tend to be more empathetic in general

00:14:50.950 --> 00:14:55.153
respond more or less to
these kinds of interventions.

00:14:57.420 --> 00:15:01.070
So on the first question,
again, the idea here is,

00:15:01.070 --> 00:15:05.000
are there interactions
between providing information

00:15:05.000 --> 00:15:08.110
and then trying to also
do this perspective,

00:15:08.110 --> 00:15:10.990
taking perspective getting
to generate empathy?

00:15:10.990 --> 00:15:14.370
And what we expected
going into this project

00:15:14.370 --> 00:15:18.350
is that if you embed
information correction

00:15:18.350 --> 00:15:20.560
in an empathy inducing narrative,

00:15:20.560 --> 00:15:22.610
that this might help
guard against the negative

00:15:22.610 --> 00:15:25.000
externalities of information
that I just discussed

00:15:25.000 --> 00:15:27.950
or you might inadvertently prime threats,

00:15:27.950 --> 00:15:30.760
but also then help with
information updating,

00:15:30.760 --> 00:15:33.550
so get people to open up and accept

00:15:33.550 --> 00:15:36.430
that their beliefs were
inaccurate and they update.

00:15:36.430 --> 00:15:38.390
And then perhaps actually incorporate that

00:15:38.390 --> 00:15:41.133
into how they're thinking
about refugees more generally.

00:15:42.197 --> 00:15:45.270
And there are two reasons that in our mind

00:15:45.270 --> 00:15:48.380
this may have this may have occurred.

00:15:48.380 --> 00:15:50.780
So the first is that emotions do affect

00:15:50.780 --> 00:15:53.490
how individuals integrate new information

00:15:53.490 --> 00:15:55.710
into their attitudes.

00:15:55.710 --> 00:15:57.990
This recent paper, for
instance, shows that

00:15:57.990 --> 00:15:59.440
when people get angry,

00:15:59.440 --> 00:16:02.270
they're less likely to
accept new information

00:16:02.270 --> 00:16:04.100
that corrects a misconception

00:16:04.100 --> 00:16:07.123
and that this has effects
on their political views.

00:16:08.120 --> 00:16:11.770
And the second is that
the cognitive demand

00:16:11.770 --> 00:16:14.020
of taking someone else's perspective,

00:16:14.020 --> 00:16:15.910
or at least reflecting on it

00:16:15.910 --> 00:16:18.490
can reduce reliance on stereotyping.

00:16:18.490 --> 00:16:20.070
And so in this case,

00:16:20.070 --> 00:16:22.840
when people get information
that counters a stereotype

00:16:22.840 --> 00:16:25.040
or misconception they have,

00:16:25.040 --> 00:16:27.830
they might be more
likely to then apply that

00:16:27.830 --> 00:16:30.060
information and update their views.

00:16:30.060 --> 00:16:32.290
And so these two
mechanisms to us suggested

00:16:32.290 --> 00:16:34.630
that it might be possible to

00:16:34.630 --> 00:16:36.540
essentially turn information provision

00:16:36.540 --> 00:16:38.090
into a more consistent,

00:16:38.090 --> 00:16:41.120
effective prejudice reduction
strategy by pairing it with

00:16:42.010 --> 00:16:44.610
a narrative that at the
same time generates empathy

00:16:44.610 --> 00:16:46.363
for a member of the out-group.

00:16:49.000 --> 00:16:50.670
On the second question, which is, again,

00:16:50.670 --> 00:16:53.120
whether these empathy
generating interventions

00:16:54.200 --> 00:16:56.690
are more or less effective for individuals

00:16:56.690 --> 00:16:59.740
who have higher or lower baseline empathy.

00:16:59.740 --> 00:17:04.110
We went into this with somewhat
ambiguous expectations for

00:17:04.110 --> 00:17:05.163
what might happen.

00:17:06.100 --> 00:17:08.070
So there's actually research showing that

00:17:08.070 --> 00:17:10.930
individuals with higher levels of empathy

00:17:10.930 --> 00:17:14.540
who just tend to feel more
empathetic towards others

00:17:14.540 --> 00:17:17.730
on a day to day basis might
actually have more empathy

00:17:17.730 --> 00:17:19.400
specifically for in-group members

00:17:19.400 --> 00:17:22.210
because those are the people
they relate most closely to.

00:17:22.210 --> 00:17:23.720
And so when they're feeling empathetic,

00:17:23.720 --> 00:17:25.970
that's mostly directed towards

00:17:25.970 --> 00:17:28.180
someone they can relate to most closely.

00:17:28.180 --> 00:17:30.280
And this might actually
have negative consequences

00:17:30.280 --> 00:17:32.350
for inter-group relations.

00:17:32.350 --> 00:17:34.370
If that empathy for their in-group

00:17:34.370 --> 00:17:37.620
then essentially causes
negative views of the out-group

00:17:37.620 --> 00:17:39.350
that they might perceive to be threatening

00:17:39.350 --> 00:17:41.100
their in-group members.

00:17:41.100 --> 00:17:42.310
And in this case,

00:17:42.310 --> 00:17:44.950
it may be that when you
try to generate empathy

00:17:44.950 --> 00:17:46.670
through these other interventions,

00:17:46.670 --> 00:17:48.460
there's somewhat of a boomerang where

00:17:48.460 --> 00:17:51.080
that empathy gets directed
towards the in-group,

00:17:51.080 --> 00:17:53.610
and then these interventions
are actually less effective

00:17:53.610 --> 00:17:57.710
for those with higher
levels of baseline empathy.

00:17:57.710 --> 00:17:58.543
On the other hand,

00:17:58.543 --> 00:18:02.330
if you are someone who is just
generally more empathetic,

00:18:02.330 --> 00:18:05.240
that's probably going to make you better

00:18:05.240 --> 00:18:07.090
at thinking about what it would be like

00:18:07.090 --> 00:18:08.950
to be in someone else's shoes

00:18:08.950 --> 00:18:13.140
and the ability to do
that might then create

00:18:13.140 --> 00:18:17.470
more openness to updating your
views of out-group members.

00:18:17.470 --> 00:18:18.303
And in that case,

00:18:18.303 --> 00:18:20.780
we might expect that these
sorts of interventions

00:18:20.780 --> 00:18:22.990
are actually more effective

00:18:22.990 --> 00:18:27.143
among individuals with a higher
baseline level of empathy.

00:18:28.680 --> 00:18:30.510
So just to step back and summarize

00:18:30.510 --> 00:18:33.980
then what we're trying
to do in this study.

00:18:33.980 --> 00:18:36.610
We're first interested in
seeing if we can identify

00:18:36.610 --> 00:18:38.770
misconceptions that are actually relevant

00:18:38.770 --> 00:18:42.720
to how Americans develop their
attitudes towards refugees.

00:18:42.720 --> 00:18:46.390
So find that information that
people don't really know,

00:18:46.390 --> 00:18:49.260
and that also should have an impact on

00:18:49.260 --> 00:18:53.880
their views of the refugee
population in the U.S.

00:18:53.880 --> 00:18:57.690
And then to see if in
correcting that misconception,

00:18:57.690 --> 00:19:02.690
we can first actually get them
to update their false view,

00:19:02.880 --> 00:19:05.460
and then second try more generally

00:19:05.460 --> 00:19:08.470
to have that spill over
into prejudice reduction

00:19:08.470 --> 00:19:12.533
and an improvement of
attitudes towards refugees.

00:19:13.920 --> 00:19:15.330
And then the third is

00:19:15.330 --> 00:19:17.180
whether we can make
that even more effective

00:19:17.180 --> 00:19:20.010
again by essentially
shielding the information

00:19:20.010 --> 00:19:22.120
in an empathy intervention,

00:19:22.120 --> 00:19:26.100
so looking for that interaction
between this information

00:19:26.100 --> 00:19:29.093
provision and using perspective getting.

00:19:30.300 --> 00:19:31.740
And then finally looking at that

00:19:31.740 --> 00:19:33.080
heterogeneous treatment effect,

00:19:33.080 --> 00:19:36.980
so does this empathy treatment
also work more or less,

00:19:36.980 --> 00:19:41.403
well, based on how much
empathy someone has in general?

00:19:43.270 --> 00:19:48.133
So any questions at this point
before I get into the design?

00:19:51.680 --> 00:19:55.890
Okay, so yeah, feel free to
interrupt if anything comes up.

00:19:55.890 --> 00:19:59.410
So we try to answer these
questions through two surveys

00:19:59.410 --> 00:20:01.970
that were done two years apart,

00:20:01.970 --> 00:20:04.000
the first in the fall of 2019

00:20:04.000 --> 00:20:07.270
with an n of approximately 3,500,

00:20:07.270 --> 00:20:09.940
and the second in the fall of 2021,

00:20:09.940 --> 00:20:14.100
so just recently with an
n of approximately 9,000.

00:20:14.100 --> 00:20:15.550
These were not panel surveys,

00:20:15.550 --> 00:20:18.410
so these are not the same individuals,

00:20:18.410 --> 00:20:22.100
but they are samples
recruited through Lucid

00:20:22.100 --> 00:20:26.560
which provides scholars
with an easy tool to

00:20:27.920 --> 00:20:29.480
get surveys done online.

00:20:29.480 --> 00:20:32.160
We feel this through Qualtrics online,

00:20:32.160 --> 00:20:35.390
and they're representative
of the American population

00:20:35.390 --> 00:20:39.200
on key demographic characteristics,
age, gender, income,

00:20:39.200 --> 00:20:43.470
ethnicity, education,
region, and partisanship.

00:20:43.470 --> 00:20:45.010
So these are not the

00:20:45.010 --> 00:20:48.220
highest quality nationally
representative samples,

00:20:48.220 --> 00:20:50.730
but they are representative
on certain characteristics

00:20:50.730 --> 00:20:52.880
that hopefully let us
draw some conclusions

00:20:53.790 --> 00:20:56.883
to generalize the American
population broadly.

00:20:59.130 --> 00:21:02.150
The first study was
intended to measure baseline

00:21:02.150 --> 00:21:05.340
knowledge and attitudes of respondents

00:21:05.340 --> 00:21:08.590
about refugees as well as
immigrants in the United States.

00:21:08.590 --> 00:21:11.350
And as I alluded to the goal here was to

00:21:11.350 --> 00:21:14.230
identify important
misconceptions about refugees.

00:21:14.230 --> 00:21:17.860
So we use this first
survey to simply see if

00:21:17.860 --> 00:21:19.640
there are certain areas in which people

00:21:19.640 --> 00:21:22.250
are particularly likely to be misinformed

00:21:22.250 --> 00:21:25.040
about what the refugee
population looks like

00:21:25.040 --> 00:21:26.453
in the United States.

00:21:27.820 --> 00:21:31.300
And then the second study
included our information,

00:21:31.300 --> 00:21:32.650
correction experiments,

00:21:32.650 --> 00:21:35.410
along with that empathy intervention,

00:21:35.410 --> 00:21:37.030
trying to build from the first study

00:21:37.030 --> 00:21:40.260
to see if correcting the
misinformation from study one

00:21:40.260 --> 00:21:44.583
actually has beneficial
effects on views of refugees.

00:21:46.920 --> 00:21:49.440
So I'll somewhat briefly
go over the results

00:21:49.440 --> 00:21:51.260
from the first study.

00:21:51.260 --> 00:21:55.350
This is a table showing some
of the questions we asked

00:21:55.350 --> 00:21:57.820
in terms of trying to get
people to at least guess

00:21:57.820 --> 00:21:59.140
or say what they thought

00:22:00.390 --> 00:22:02.853
the refugee population
in the U.S looks like.

00:22:03.990 --> 00:22:08.870
So in terms of how many refugees
were admitted that year,

00:22:08.870 --> 00:22:12.440
the median response was
20,000 and that's actually

00:22:12.440 --> 00:22:14.510
remarkably close to the actual number

00:22:14.510 --> 00:22:18.620
which was around 22,000 in 2019.

00:22:18.620 --> 00:22:20.300
In terms of the percent of refugees

00:22:20.300 --> 00:22:23.900
coming into the United States
who are women and children,

00:22:23.900 --> 00:22:26.960
it's a slight underestimate
of about 10 percentage points,

00:22:26.960 --> 00:22:29.810
but a majority of respondents

00:22:29.810 --> 00:22:32.730
or an on average respondents
thought that about 60%

00:22:32.730 --> 00:22:34.840
of refugees were women and children

00:22:34.840 --> 00:22:37.440
compared to the actual percentage of 70,

00:22:37.440 --> 00:22:39.653
between 2015 and 2017.

00:22:40.601 --> 00:22:43.410
There actually was not
really an overestimation

00:22:43.410 --> 00:22:45.690
of the percent that was Muslim,

00:22:45.690 --> 00:22:50.690
so looking at the years,
2002 to 2016, on average,

00:22:50.720 --> 00:22:54.310
about 32% of refugees admitted were Muslim

00:22:54.310 --> 00:22:58.770
and the average guess for
our respondents was 28%.

00:22:58.770 --> 00:23:03.420
This did drop under Trump to
16, but I think in general,

00:23:03.420 --> 00:23:06.010
this is pretty close to what
it was for a long period.

00:23:06.010 --> 00:23:06.970
And then there is a slight,

00:23:06.970 --> 00:23:09.070
there is an underestimate on how many

00:23:09.070 --> 00:23:10.550
Christians are admitted especially

00:23:10.550 --> 00:23:13.513
for those years from 2002 to 2016.

00:23:15.460 --> 00:23:16.790
In terms of English fluency,

00:23:16.790 --> 00:23:20.200
we asked respondents to
basically add up to 100

00:23:20.200 --> 00:23:22.640
in terms of how many
one percent of refugees

00:23:22.640 --> 00:23:24.640
spoke English fluently when they arrived,

00:23:24.640 --> 00:23:26.970
spoke some English or spoke no English,

00:23:26.970 --> 00:23:28.900
and here respondents
were remarkably close,

00:23:28.900 --> 00:23:31.320
at least on average.

00:23:31.320 --> 00:23:33.330
So a slight overestimate actually

00:23:33.330 --> 00:23:36.190
of how many speak English
fluently on arrival

00:23:36.190 --> 00:23:40.980
and a slight underestimate
of how many speak no English.

00:23:40.980 --> 00:23:44.010
In terms of whether refugees pay taxes,

00:23:44.010 --> 00:23:45.890
the modal answer was not sure,

00:23:45.890 --> 00:23:49.670
but more said yes than no
which is the correct response.

00:23:49.670 --> 00:23:54.080
And so it's really when we get
to these security issues that

00:23:54.080 --> 00:23:57.830
we start to see really
large divergences from

00:23:57.830 --> 00:24:00.763
what the actual refugee
population looks like.

00:24:02.300 --> 00:24:04.260
So we ask people the percent

00:24:04.260 --> 00:24:07.900
of terrorist attacks since
1980, for instance, that

00:24:07.900 --> 00:24:09.650
were conducted by refugees.

00:24:09.650 --> 00:24:11.920
The actual answer is zero.

00:24:11.920 --> 00:24:14.440
And the average response here was 29%,

00:24:14.440 --> 00:24:16.360
so this is a big miss.

00:24:16.360 --> 00:24:19.940
We also asked respondents how many months

00:24:19.940 --> 00:24:22.590
of vetting the typical
refugee goes through?

00:24:22.590 --> 00:24:24.470
The modal response was 6 to 12 months.

00:24:24.470 --> 00:24:29.020
The actual response, the actual
answer is 18 to 24 months.

00:24:29.020 --> 00:24:32.580
And this is also a pretty
substantial difference.

00:24:32.580 --> 00:24:35.300
In terms of the likelihood that
refugees would commit crime,

00:24:35.300 --> 00:24:38.190
it's actually, most respondents

00:24:38.190 --> 00:24:41.220
did acknowledge that it
was an equal likelihood

00:24:41.220 --> 00:24:44.070
and there is a bit of a
miss to origin countries,

00:24:44.070 --> 00:24:47.100
so most said Mexico
and Syria would provide

00:24:47.100 --> 00:24:48.960
the most refugees in
the year of the survey

00:24:48.960 --> 00:24:52.450
when in fact it was the
DRC, Burma and Ukraine.

00:24:52.450 --> 00:24:54.840
So there are clearly misconceptions here.

00:24:54.840 --> 00:24:58.040
Some of this information is quite specific

00:24:58.040 --> 00:25:00.320
and you might not expect
everyone to know it,

00:25:00.320 --> 00:25:04.870
but even in those cases people
are getting pretty close.

00:25:04.870 --> 00:25:08.870
But what we do seem to see
is a big miss in terms of how

00:25:08.870 --> 00:25:11.800
much refugees are tied
up into security threats.

00:25:11.800 --> 00:25:13.940
And so just to provide a
little more detail on that,

00:25:13.940 --> 00:25:16.940
this is the distribution of responses on

00:25:16.940 --> 00:25:19.260
the months of vetting
that refugees go through.

00:25:19.260 --> 00:25:22.730
So again, the true answer
is here at 18 to 24 months.

00:25:22.730 --> 00:25:25.650
Most of the distribution is
quite a bit lower than that,

00:25:25.650 --> 00:25:28.233
especially with the mode 6 to 12.

00:25:29.580 --> 00:25:31.860
And then again, there's a pretty big miss

00:25:31.860 --> 00:25:33.870
in terms of the percent
of terrorist activity

00:25:33.870 --> 00:25:37.990
from 1980 to the present
conducted by refugees.

00:25:37.990 --> 00:25:40.380
So there are many people saying zero,

00:25:40.380 --> 00:25:45.373
but quite a few are way
overestimating that percent.

00:25:48.590 --> 00:25:50.060
And this is correlational,

00:25:50.060 --> 00:25:53.010
so we can only take so much from it,

00:25:53.010 --> 00:25:56.400
but there is some evidence that those who

00:25:56.400 --> 00:26:00.370
miss on this security threat of refugees,

00:26:00.370 --> 00:26:03.550
those who have the highest
misconceptions here

00:26:03.550 --> 00:26:06.760
are much less likely to
have favorable attitude

00:26:06.760 --> 00:26:09.070
toward refugees as you might expect.

00:26:09.070 --> 00:26:11.330
So in terms of the feeling thermometer,

00:26:11.330 --> 00:26:14.410
they're much less likely
to rate refugees formally.

00:26:14.410 --> 00:26:16.610
They're more likely to
also say that refugees

00:26:16.610 --> 00:26:18.210
might threaten national identity,

00:26:18.210 --> 00:26:22.640
increase the risk of terrorism
and are a burden on the U.S.

00:26:22.640 --> 00:26:24.690
We also in this survey

00:26:24.690 --> 00:26:28.570
had to question where
respondents could donate money to

00:26:28.570 --> 00:26:32.800
the Refugee Council USA or
another pro refugee group

00:26:32.800 --> 00:26:37.800
or an organization that's been
active in promoting stricter

00:26:38.700 --> 00:26:40.180
limits on refugees,

00:26:40.180 --> 00:26:42.260
and again you see that individuals who

00:26:42.260 --> 00:26:44.500
are more likely hold this misconception

00:26:44.500 --> 00:26:48.020
are much less likely to donate
to the pro refugee groups.

00:26:48.020 --> 00:26:51.750
And then finally like also
with policy preferences,

00:26:51.750 --> 00:26:54.430
those who believe that refugees

00:26:54.430 --> 00:26:55.690
don't go through as much vetting

00:26:55.690 --> 00:26:58.220
or commit more terrorist attacks

00:26:58.220 --> 00:27:00.870
are more supportive of the Muslim ban,

00:27:00.870 --> 00:27:03.500
want more screening, want fewer refugees.

00:27:03.500 --> 00:27:05.640
So this is not conclusive.

00:27:05.640 --> 00:27:07.560
This may be reverse causation,

00:27:07.560 --> 00:27:09.830
but it does suggest that perhaps

00:27:09.830 --> 00:27:12.890
these security misconceptions are related

00:27:12.890 --> 00:27:15.220
to how people are
developing their attitudes

00:27:15.220 --> 00:27:17.983
towards refugees in the United States.

00:27:20.350 --> 00:27:21.543
I see a hand.

00:27:22.780 --> 00:27:26.300
- [Susan] Yeah, I was
wondering about some of the...

00:27:27.210 --> 00:27:30.530
Some of the information that you compared

00:27:30.530 --> 00:27:33.830
the attitudes to the factual information,

00:27:33.830 --> 00:27:38.633
like for example, what struck me, refugees

00:27:41.380 --> 00:27:46.380
engaging crime, having equal
likelihood of engaging in crime

00:27:47.060 --> 00:27:49.730
and, you know, I don't
know that that, you know,

00:27:49.730 --> 00:27:52.510
I've always heard otherwise.

00:27:52.510 --> 00:27:56.980
And so, and there were a
couple of other things also

00:27:56.980 --> 00:27:59.720
that you mentioned that
seemed off the mark,

00:27:59.720 --> 00:28:01.333
but maybe I'm misinformed.

00:28:02.500 --> 00:28:05.690
So I was wondering where you
got your information from.

00:28:05.690 --> 00:28:08.850
- Yeah, so we actually looked
at a variety of sources

00:28:08.850 --> 00:28:10.710
to make sure we have this right.

00:28:10.710 --> 00:28:14.100
And frankly, several of these answers,

00:28:14.100 --> 00:28:16.370
I would not have known exactly myself.

00:28:16.370 --> 00:28:21.010
And so I think our idea here
is not that not to show that,

00:28:21.010 --> 00:28:24.410
you know, this is a fairly
technical specific policy issue,

00:28:24.410 --> 00:28:26.490
and to say that people should know exactly

00:28:26.490 --> 00:28:28.830
what percent of refugees
are women and children,

00:28:28.830 --> 00:28:32.900
or if refugees are equally
likely to commit crime

00:28:32.900 --> 00:28:35.200
or less so or more so.

00:28:35.200 --> 00:28:38.270
But in general, some of
these misconceptions,

00:28:38.270 --> 00:28:39.990
if they're really large,

00:28:39.990 --> 00:28:42.850
might be contributing to how people are

00:28:42.850 --> 00:28:45.432
perceiving refugees as threats or not.

00:28:45.432 --> 00:28:48.880
And so the fact that people
on average aren't so far off,

00:28:48.880 --> 00:28:52.710
we thought was positive for some of these.

00:28:52.710 --> 00:28:53.890
On terms of the crime,

00:28:53.890 --> 00:28:56.400
one specifically there has been,

00:28:56.400 --> 00:28:58.880
there have been recent papers published

00:28:58.880 --> 00:29:01.210
looking at this question with

00:29:01.210 --> 00:29:02.830
pretty rigorous research designs,

00:29:02.830 --> 00:29:06.010
and basically what they're showing is that

00:29:06.010 --> 00:29:07.823
as refugees come into an area,

00:29:08.710 --> 00:29:11.520
there's a precisely estimated null effect

00:29:11.520 --> 00:29:12.983
on crime rates increasing.

00:29:14.000 --> 00:29:16.740
I think this is pretty
much the conclusion from

00:29:18.220 --> 00:29:19.460
other sources as well.

00:29:19.460 --> 00:29:21.330
I don't remember the exact citation,

00:29:21.330 --> 00:29:24.083
but if you're interested,
I can provide it to you.

00:29:25.680 --> 00:29:27.920
Likewise, with the women and
children, Muslim, Christian,

00:29:27.920 --> 00:29:29.560
this we were taking directly from

00:29:29.560 --> 00:29:32.873
U.S government statistics online.

00:29:35.600 --> 00:29:36.433
- Thank you.

00:29:39.810 --> 00:29:44.520
- Yes, I have a question also,

00:29:44.520 --> 00:29:49.520
I mean, if this information
is divided by like

00:29:49.940 --> 00:29:54.940
political affiliation and
also a bit related to that

00:29:55.930 --> 00:29:59.000
is that, I mean,

00:29:59.000 --> 00:30:04.000
I think it has become harder
for people conducting surveys

00:30:05.740 --> 00:30:08.403
to sort to get to the,

00:30:09.980 --> 00:30:14.080
the way people actually think if you are,

00:30:14.080 --> 00:30:16.653
as, for example, their voting behavior,

00:30:17.580 --> 00:30:21.570
I mean, they have been
quite some mismatches

00:30:21.570 --> 00:30:25.640
on what people say and
what they actually do.

00:30:25.640 --> 00:30:28.530
So I wonder if some of the information

00:30:28.530 --> 00:30:31.180
that you are getting yourself might

00:30:32.120 --> 00:30:35.553
experience the same problem as well.

00:30:36.580 --> 00:30:38.710
- Yeah, that's absolutely a possibility.

00:30:38.710 --> 00:30:40.910
So your first part of the question,

00:30:40.910 --> 00:30:43.090
there are definitely
differences here, for instance,

00:30:43.090 --> 00:30:44.770
between Republicans and Democrats

00:30:46.340 --> 00:30:49.970
in estimating these differences,

00:30:49.970 --> 00:30:52.530
like differences on crime
or terrorist activity,

00:30:52.530 --> 00:30:54.540
or how many refugees are admitted

00:30:54.540 --> 00:30:56.450
or the women and children, et cetera.

00:30:56.450 --> 00:30:58.610
And part of that may be

00:31:00.060 --> 00:31:02.720
answering in a way that justifies

00:31:02.720 --> 00:31:04.540
more restrictive policies because

00:31:04.540 --> 00:31:06.813
you prefer those restrictive
policies and then

00:31:06.813 --> 00:31:10.850
want to answer in a way
that supports the policies.

00:31:10.850 --> 00:31:12.500
Whereas Democrats might do the opposite,

00:31:12.500 --> 00:31:15.920
which is that they want
more refugees admitted,

00:31:15.920 --> 00:31:17.410
so even if they don't know the answer,

00:31:17.410 --> 00:31:19.980
they're gonna just guess a low number

00:31:19.980 --> 00:31:22.410
on the terrorist activity
question, for instance,

00:31:22.410 --> 00:31:25.090
because that aligns with
the view they already hold.

00:31:25.090 --> 00:31:28.070
So we're taking an agnostic stance on

00:31:28.070 --> 00:31:31.080
whether this information is actually

00:31:32.200 --> 00:31:34.640
reflects I guess you could
say their true beliefs.

00:31:34.640 --> 00:31:37.370
We did not incentivize them
to definitely get it right,

00:31:37.370 --> 00:31:39.840
we were just curious in
what they would tell us

00:31:39.840 --> 00:31:41.133
their conceptions were,

00:31:42.250 --> 00:31:44.050
and that's somewhat relevant to

00:31:44.050 --> 00:31:45.580
what we then do in the second study

00:31:45.580 --> 00:31:48.340
and how we set up whether
information provision

00:31:48.340 --> 00:31:50.510
can actually reduce prejudice.

00:31:50.510 --> 00:31:52.323
Because one of the reasons that,

00:31:54.160 --> 00:31:57.830
you know, it may fail to
have an effect is because

00:31:57.830 --> 00:31:59.870
even if the information
is new and relevant,

00:31:59.870 --> 00:32:01.993
people just aren't processing it,

00:32:03.150 --> 00:32:05.930
and we to preview a little bit,
that's kind of what we find.

00:32:05.930 --> 00:32:09.660
So even though we basically update people

00:32:09.660 --> 00:32:10.530
on months of vetting

00:32:10.530 --> 00:32:13.140
and it doesn't seem to
have any spillover effects,

00:32:13.140 --> 00:32:14.150
and that might suggest

00:32:14.150 --> 00:32:16.180
that when people answer this question,

00:32:16.180 --> 00:32:18.040
they're just providing the answer

00:32:18.040 --> 00:32:20.360
that already aligns with their views.

00:32:20.360 --> 00:32:22.950
Maybe they have a sense of the truth

00:32:22.950 --> 00:32:24.780
and just don't wanna say it,

00:32:24.780 --> 00:32:26.330
or maybe they don't just don't care.

00:32:26.330 --> 00:32:28.880
And so it could be,

00:32:28.880 --> 00:32:32.560
it's probably the case that
some of these answers are

00:32:32.560 --> 00:32:35.170
driven by other considerations.

00:32:35.170 --> 00:32:38.060
Because again, I wouldn't
expect most people

00:32:38.060 --> 00:32:41.290
to have thought deeply about
the specifics of these issues.

00:32:41.290 --> 00:32:43.450
That's probably asking too much.

00:32:43.450 --> 00:32:46.440
And so where they draw their priors on

00:32:46.440 --> 00:32:49.140
are gonna be informed by a
bunch of different sources.

00:32:51.040 --> 00:32:51.873
- [Teresa] Thanks.

00:32:56.050 --> 00:32:58.760
- So if there aren't
other questions there,

00:32:58.760 --> 00:33:00.140
but although I'm happy to take them,

00:33:00.140 --> 00:33:02.560
I'll move on to the second study.

00:33:02.560 --> 00:33:06.660
Again, the idea here being
that we build from the first

00:33:06.660 --> 00:33:08.770
to try and see if correcting

00:33:08.770 --> 00:33:11.120
a misconception that
stands out in the survey

00:33:11.120 --> 00:33:14.840
can have effects on
attitudes towards refugees.

00:33:14.840 --> 00:33:16.040
So the structure of the survey,

00:33:16.040 --> 00:33:18.560
we first ask demographic questions

00:33:18.560 --> 00:33:21.560
and then refugee security priors again.

00:33:21.560 --> 00:33:23.750
So we basically ask some
of the questions from

00:33:23.750 --> 00:33:26.260
the first survey that I just talked about.

00:33:26.260 --> 00:33:28.180
We then try to measure baseline empathy

00:33:28.180 --> 00:33:30.370
using a questionnaire from Davis.

00:33:30.370 --> 00:33:32.280
This is an old source about

00:33:32.280 --> 00:33:35.060
how to measure someone's
general levels of empathy

00:33:35.060 --> 00:33:38.290
to get at those heterogeneous
treatment effects I mentioned.

00:33:38.290 --> 00:33:41.640
And then we assign people to
treatment groups or control,

00:33:41.640 --> 00:33:43.640
followed by measurement of the outcomes.

00:33:45.050 --> 00:33:49.290
So we have three treatment
groups here and a control group.

00:33:49.290 --> 00:33:50.420
So the first treatment,

00:33:50.420 --> 00:33:52.570
and I'll talk about these in more detail

00:33:52.570 --> 00:33:53.790
is that correction

00:33:53.790 --> 00:33:56.363
on vetting and security
checks specifically,

00:33:57.580 --> 00:34:00.740
the second is providing a perspective,

00:34:00.740 --> 00:34:03.470
getting narrative about a refugee.

00:34:03.470 --> 00:34:04.900
And the third is simply combining

00:34:04.900 --> 00:34:06.410
those two treatments together

00:34:06.410 --> 00:34:09.740
to see if there is a
beneficial interaction here.

00:34:09.740 --> 00:34:11.660
The control is a pure control,

00:34:11.660 --> 00:34:13.920
so respondents don't
get assigned to anything

00:34:13.920 --> 00:34:15.870
and they just proceed through the survey

00:34:15.870 --> 00:34:17.273
to the outcome questions.

00:34:18.980 --> 00:34:21.270
So this is what the information
treatment looks like.

00:34:21.270 --> 00:34:25.620
Essentially, we chose to focus
on the vetting misconception,

00:34:25.620 --> 00:34:28.310
where people are underestimating

00:34:28.310 --> 00:34:31.670
how much refugees go
through security checks.

00:34:31.670 --> 00:34:33.580
The idea being here that we could provide

00:34:33.580 --> 00:34:35.610
the most credible
information to respondents

00:34:35.610 --> 00:34:39.020
by citing government sources directly.

00:34:39.020 --> 00:34:40.590
We thought this might have more weight

00:34:40.590 --> 00:34:43.810
especially given that many of
the organizations mentioned

00:34:43.810 --> 00:34:46.720
have fairly high levels
of trust in the U.S.

00:34:46.720 --> 00:34:48.370
So we wanted to try
and make this treatment

00:34:48.370 --> 00:34:49.330
as strong as possible

00:34:49.330 --> 00:34:51.630
and thought that this
would be the way to do it.

00:34:51.630 --> 00:34:53.690
So first respondents are
told that they will read

00:34:53.690 --> 00:34:55.190
some information about the U.S

00:34:56.040 --> 00:34:57.430
and that we would then
ref have them reflect

00:34:57.430 --> 00:34:58.393
on what they read,

00:34:59.290 --> 00:35:01.230
and the treatment itself
on the next page says,

00:35:01.230 --> 00:35:04.600
according to U.S government
and non-governmental agencies,

00:35:04.600 --> 00:35:06.940
refugees undergo more extensive
and thorough screening

00:35:06.940 --> 00:35:10.000
than anyone else who travels
into the United States.

00:35:10.000 --> 00:35:12.540
Biometric and medical data
are collected from the refugee

00:35:12.540 --> 00:35:14.240
who is screened by, you know,

00:35:14.240 --> 00:35:18.420
then a whole host of security
oriented organizations.

00:35:18.420 --> 00:35:21.640
The average time for the vetting
process is 18 to 24 months,

00:35:21.640 --> 00:35:23.520
but it can take even longer,

00:35:23.520 --> 00:35:26.800
to view an infographic of
the process click here.

00:35:26.800 --> 00:35:28.780
And that would open into a new tab.

00:35:28.780 --> 00:35:30.900
So not many people actually clicked on it.

00:35:30.900 --> 00:35:32.660
This is just a little more information

00:35:32.660 --> 00:35:34.480
about what we've already told them

00:35:34.480 --> 00:35:36.380
and to give the source even more clearly

00:35:36.380 --> 00:35:39.333
if they were concerned about that.

00:35:40.230 --> 00:35:43.270
But this is the information
that most respondents

00:35:43.270 --> 00:35:45.120
in this treatment group were getting.

00:35:46.770 --> 00:35:48.370
And then for the second treatment group,

00:35:48.370 --> 00:35:51.140
we went with a perspective
getting narrative.

00:35:51.140 --> 00:35:53.500
So the idea here is that
you take a narrative

00:35:53.500 --> 00:35:56.050
from an individual of an out-group,

00:35:56.050 --> 00:35:58.670
and you explain to people

00:35:58.670 --> 00:36:01.370
what it's like to be in their shoes,

00:36:01.370 --> 00:36:03.710
give a personal story
about how hard it might be

00:36:03.710 --> 00:36:07.110
to be a refugee coming
to the United States.

00:36:07.110 --> 00:36:10.020
We chose to go with a
hypothetical story here,

00:36:10.020 --> 00:36:13.233
and we told respondents that
because we didn't want them,

00:36:14.210 --> 00:36:18.763
we didn't want to
essentially use deception,

00:36:19.940 --> 00:36:22.210
but what we did and I'll
talk about this a little more

00:36:22.210 --> 00:36:24.550
is draw on real stories
to create this story.

00:36:24.550 --> 00:36:26.220
The reason we wanted
to do the hypothetical

00:36:26.220 --> 00:36:27.960
was to have as much control as possible

00:36:27.960 --> 00:36:29.093
over the information.

00:36:29.990 --> 00:36:31.710
So we have this story about Abdi,

00:36:31.710 --> 00:36:36.310
who we say was once one of 26
million refugees in the world.

00:36:36.310 --> 00:36:40.300
He flees war-torn Somalia
at a young age for Kenya

00:36:40.300 --> 00:36:42.300
and then lives in a camp for nine years.

00:36:43.550 --> 00:36:45.150
He says, it feels like a big jail cell.

00:36:45.150 --> 00:36:47.080
They give you food, housing
and the rest will be

00:36:47.080 --> 00:36:48.060
from the hands of countries

00:36:48.060 --> 00:36:50.060
coming to help people like America.

00:36:50.060 --> 00:36:51.410
You just pray to a Allah,

00:36:51.410 --> 00:36:53.890
when am I going to leave this place?

00:36:53.890 --> 00:36:56.750
We then talk about his
experience in the United States.

00:36:56.750 --> 00:37:00.612
So how hard it was to settle in

00:37:00.612 --> 00:37:03.300
and explain his background to people,

00:37:03.300 --> 00:37:04.867
whether that's Ramadan or Eid.

00:37:06.362 --> 00:37:09.270
Now, he has a business, he
runs his own accounting firm.

00:37:09.270 --> 00:37:10.480
He does community service

00:37:10.480 --> 00:37:12.150
and he likes music like Led Zeppelin.

00:37:12.150 --> 00:37:15.150
So the first part of this is
to try and give the experience

00:37:15.150 --> 00:37:18.320
of actually being going
through the refugee process

00:37:18.320 --> 00:37:20.450
and the second then trying to integrate

00:37:20.450 --> 00:37:22.950
and build a new life once
you've actually arrived.

00:37:27.153 --> 00:37:30.903
- By putting, I mean,
some of the information

00:37:33.080 --> 00:37:36.600
that you put about this
hypothetical individual,

00:37:36.600 --> 00:37:41.600
aren't you already sort of
bringing up some of the biases

00:37:44.150 --> 00:37:46.000
that people might have as well?

00:37:46.000 --> 00:37:48.393
And then, so for example, I don't know,

00:37:48.393 --> 00:37:52.360
one of the things that
they do in the labor market

00:37:52.360 --> 00:37:55.640
is that they put anonymous their name,

00:37:55.640 --> 00:37:58.680
because then if you associate certain name

00:37:58.680 --> 00:38:01.540
with certain characteristics
of the people.

00:38:01.540 --> 00:38:03.000
So obviously here you want

00:38:04.000 --> 00:38:08.143
to get sort of the more of empathy

00:38:08.143 --> 00:38:10.270
of what is the process to go through,

00:38:10.270 --> 00:38:13.350
but are you also sort of, as I said,

00:38:13.350 --> 00:38:16.579
the people that are already
have their own biases

00:38:16.579 --> 00:38:20.323
bringing that or people
who have doubts about that.

00:38:21.160 --> 00:38:23.453
- Yeah, that's definitely a possibility.

00:38:25.150 --> 00:38:28.850
So for instance saying that he's Muslim,

00:38:28.850 --> 00:38:30.140
there's lots of research showing that

00:38:30.140 --> 00:38:32.450
people react more negatively to

00:38:32.450 --> 00:38:35.760
immigrants who are
non-Christian in the U.S

00:38:35.760 --> 00:38:37.743
that might prompt this,

00:38:38.780 --> 00:38:41.380
you mentioned the economic competition.

00:38:41.380 --> 00:38:43.290
That might also be the case.

00:38:43.290 --> 00:38:45.350
But I think what the
prior research suggests

00:38:45.350 --> 00:38:47.000
is that when you really humanize

00:38:47.880 --> 00:38:52.150
people that often have
face negative stereotypes,

00:38:52.150 --> 00:38:53.520
it seems like on average,

00:38:53.520 --> 00:38:56.860
this is beneficial and that
is actually what we find.

00:38:56.860 --> 00:38:58.833
And on the hypothetical part,

00:39:00.270 --> 00:39:02.360
you know, if anything that
might weaken the treatment,

00:39:02.360 --> 00:39:04.070
if they're like, well,
this isn't a real person,

00:39:04.070 --> 00:39:05.610
why should I care?

00:39:05.610 --> 00:39:07.300
So, whereas the information treatment

00:39:07.300 --> 00:39:09.740
we tried to make as strong as possible,

00:39:09.740 --> 00:39:12.220
this one we actually were okay with it

00:39:12.220 --> 00:39:13.360
potentially being weaker.

00:39:13.360 --> 00:39:16.070
And that's partly because
previous literature shows

00:39:16.070 --> 00:39:18.800
that this sort of approach is
supposed to be more effective.

00:39:18.800 --> 00:39:20.250
And we were really trying to see like,

00:39:20.250 --> 00:39:24.083
can we make this information
provision helpful or not?

00:39:26.050 --> 00:39:28.760
And so I'll go into this a little more.

00:39:28.760 --> 00:39:31.100
The combined treatment essentially puts in

00:39:31.100 --> 00:39:32.580
the information paragraph

00:39:32.580 --> 00:39:35.638
after the part where Abdi is talking about

00:39:35.638 --> 00:39:40.638
how it was coming to the
U.S, how long he had to wait.

00:39:40.830 --> 00:39:42.720
This felt like a natural point

00:39:42.720 --> 00:39:44.550
and then proceeded again to

00:39:44.550 --> 00:39:46.400
what it was like trying to integrate.

00:39:48.080 --> 00:39:50.390
So again, these are
hypothetical individuals,

00:39:50.390 --> 00:39:53.870
but we did base these on
stories from real articles.

00:39:53.870 --> 00:39:55.260
There were two articles

00:39:55.260 --> 00:39:57.250
from the Courier-Journal in Louisville,

00:39:57.250 --> 00:39:59.750
and then from NPR.

00:39:59.750 --> 00:40:01.290
The first was about Louisville

00:40:01.290 --> 00:40:02.700
immigrant share stories of terror,

00:40:02.700 --> 00:40:04.970
love and courage on the road to America,

00:40:04.970 --> 00:40:08.060
the NPR one, Muslims in America
telling their own stories.

00:40:08.060 --> 00:40:10.920
And these articles are
essentially doing exactly

00:40:10.920 --> 00:40:12.680
what our treatment is supposed to do,

00:40:12.680 --> 00:40:14.930
which is giving people this perspective,

00:40:14.930 --> 00:40:19.570
humanizing both Muslim
Americans and immigrants.

00:40:19.570 --> 00:40:22.325
And we actually borrow real quotes from

00:40:22.325 --> 00:40:24.500
these articles in real
parts of the stories.

00:40:24.500 --> 00:40:26.760
So the Led Zeppelin part for instance,

00:40:26.760 --> 00:40:28.880
was one of these stories,

00:40:28.880 --> 00:40:31.230
the quotes about waiting
to come to the U.S

00:40:31.230 --> 00:40:34.440
for all those years also were
from one of these stories,

00:40:34.440 --> 00:40:37.940
going to Somalia at a young
age, losing parents, et cetera.

00:40:37.940 --> 00:40:41.330
And so, you know, we
felt that that would be

00:40:41.330 --> 00:40:43.330
most likely to generate this empathy

00:40:43.330 --> 00:40:45.870
and humanize the individual,

00:40:45.870 --> 00:40:48.020
but also to avoid deception

00:40:48.020 --> 00:40:49.630
and just give credit to these articles,

00:40:49.630 --> 00:40:52.560
we then did have a debriefing
statement at the end,

00:40:52.560 --> 00:40:56.010
just saying that, you
know, you read this story,

00:40:56.010 --> 00:40:58.920
this was based on real people
if you want to read more,

00:40:58.920 --> 00:41:00.213
you can click here.

00:41:02.700 --> 00:41:05.410
So after respondents were assigned

00:41:05.410 --> 00:41:06.950
to one of these three treatments,

00:41:06.950 --> 00:41:08.930
or if they're in the control group,

00:41:08.930 --> 00:41:10.890
sorry, after they're assigned
to these three treatments,

00:41:10.890 --> 00:41:13.970
we then ask them to please
write a short paragraph,

00:41:13.970 --> 00:41:17.660
summarizing what you just read
and how you feel about it.

00:41:17.660 --> 00:41:20.320
So we had two purposes to doing this.

00:41:20.320 --> 00:41:24.170
The first was we wanted people to actually

00:41:24.170 --> 00:41:26.310
think about what they had just read,

00:41:26.310 --> 00:41:28.800
we wanted to make it as
likely as possible that

00:41:28.800 --> 00:41:31.580
they would really take that
information into account

00:41:31.580 --> 00:41:34.170
or really reflect on this story

00:41:34.170 --> 00:41:35.160
and what that might mean

00:41:35.160 --> 00:41:37.223
for the experience of being a refugee.

00:41:38.240 --> 00:41:41.660
But the second reason also

00:41:41.660 --> 00:41:44.120
was because we wanted this module,

00:41:44.120 --> 00:41:45.780
we wanted the treatments to feel like

00:41:45.780 --> 00:41:48.940
they could have been their
own modules in the survey,

00:41:48.940 --> 00:41:52.270
that there was a question
to be answered about

00:41:52.270 --> 00:41:53.410
reading the paragraph

00:41:53.410 --> 00:41:56.530
so that when respondents
then went on to the outcomes,

00:41:56.530 --> 00:41:59.810
it wasn't as obvious that
they had just read this story

00:41:59.810 --> 00:42:03.880
because we wanted them to answer
these subsequent questions.

00:42:03.880 --> 00:42:06.870
So we're trying to avoid
demand effects essentially

00:42:06.870 --> 00:42:10.200
by building in this
question to the treatments.

00:42:10.200 --> 00:42:12.680
So after one of the treat,
after the treatments

00:42:12.680 --> 00:42:14.780
or if they're in their control,

00:42:14.780 --> 00:42:18.620
they go to a new page and they
have four outcome measures.

00:42:18.620 --> 00:42:21.320
So first do they actually
update their prior

00:42:21.320 --> 00:42:25.240
on the extensive nature
of the vetting process?

00:42:25.240 --> 00:42:27.900
How warmly do they feel
as far as refugees?

00:42:27.900 --> 00:42:31.070
How many refugees do they
want to admit to the U.S?

00:42:31.070 --> 00:42:33.240
And then would they be
willing to write a letter

00:42:33.240 --> 00:42:36.740
to the president about their views

00:42:36.740 --> 00:42:38.860
on how many refugees should be admitted?

00:42:38.860 --> 00:42:40.530
So I'll go through each of these in turn

00:42:40.530 --> 00:42:43.393
and then show the results
FOR each of them as well.

00:42:44.370 --> 00:42:46.283
So the first question was, again,

00:42:47.210 --> 00:42:48.840
you know, approximately
how many months of vetting

00:42:48.840 --> 00:42:49.920
does a refugee grow through

00:42:49.920 --> 00:42:52.150
before being resettled
in the United States?

00:42:52.150 --> 00:42:54.360
If you do not know, please
give your best guess.

00:42:54.360 --> 00:42:55.940
So this is a question that we had asked

00:42:55.940 --> 00:42:57.630
on that very first survey.

00:42:57.630 --> 00:43:00.510
We then ask it in the
beginning of the second survey,

00:43:00.510 --> 00:43:03.880
and then we ask it again in the
end as part of the outcomes.

00:43:03.880 --> 00:43:06.410
And we want to see if people actually

00:43:06.410 --> 00:43:08.923
get the answer right on the second time.

00:43:09.940 --> 00:43:12.550
And so this is what the results look like

00:43:12.550 --> 00:43:17.100
with the distributions of
responses on the number of months

00:43:17.100 --> 00:43:21.740
for the initial answers and the prior,

00:43:21.740 --> 00:43:24.250
sorry, the prior answers
and the posterior answers

00:43:24.250 --> 00:43:26.040
after the treatments,

00:43:26.040 --> 00:43:27.440
these are the three treatment groups

00:43:27.440 --> 00:43:28.960
and this is the control.

00:43:28.960 --> 00:43:32.060
So as you might expect,
if there's no treatment,

00:43:32.060 --> 00:43:35.200
the respondents don't update
at all, why would they?

00:43:35.200 --> 00:43:38.300
The answers are pretty much identical.

00:43:38.300 --> 00:43:40.980
However, if they receive the
information we give them,

00:43:40.980 --> 00:43:43.020
they do correct quite a lot.

00:43:43.020 --> 00:43:45.850
So now the distribution is centered around

00:43:45.850 --> 00:43:47.523
the correct answer and most of,

00:43:48.820 --> 00:43:52.890
most of that distribution
is on the correct answer.

00:43:52.890 --> 00:43:53.723
For the perspective,

00:43:53.723 --> 00:43:56.080
getting treatment only
where they read this story,

00:43:56.080 --> 00:43:59.400
they don't update at all even though

00:43:59.400 --> 00:44:03.040
we talk a little bit about how
long he had the wait to come.

00:44:03.040 --> 00:44:06.190
So what I will flag here
is that we had expected

00:44:06.190 --> 00:44:08.710
that people might become more open

00:44:08.710 --> 00:44:12.170
to correcting their misconceptions

00:44:12.170 --> 00:44:16.290
if we embedded the information
in that empathy narrative.

00:44:16.290 --> 00:44:18.100
And we don't really see that

00:44:18.100 --> 00:44:20.650
essentially the effect here is the same.

00:44:20.650 --> 00:44:23.870
If the info is on its
own or in the narrative,

00:44:23.870 --> 00:44:28.013
there's no added benefit to
trying to combine the two.

00:44:30.430 --> 00:44:33.970
The second outcome is
trying to broaden out more,

00:44:33.970 --> 00:44:36.050
getting closer to prejudice

00:44:36.050 --> 00:44:39.930
or negative views of refugees
personally as people.

00:44:39.930 --> 00:44:42.310
So we use a standard feeling thermometer

00:44:42.310 --> 00:44:44.500
saying on a scale from 0 to 100,

00:44:44.500 --> 00:44:46.430
where zero equals completely unfavorable

00:44:46.430 --> 00:44:49.210
and 100 equals completely favorable,

00:44:49.210 --> 00:44:50.660
how do you feel about refugees

00:44:50.660 --> 00:44:52.453
who come to the United States?

00:44:53.600 --> 00:44:56.080
So here what we find is
in the control group,

00:44:56.080 --> 00:44:59.450
an average response of 62,

00:44:59.450 --> 00:45:03.400
and providing that information
about the vetting process

00:45:03.400 --> 00:45:05.840
doesn't really change this at all,

00:45:05.840 --> 00:45:08.250
it's also 62.6.

00:45:08.250 --> 00:45:11.680
So we might have thought
that convincing people

00:45:11.680 --> 00:45:14.637
that refugees go through
this extensive vetting

00:45:15.570 --> 00:45:18.500
would make them less worried
about security threats.

00:45:18.500 --> 00:45:21.790
And therefore they might feel
more warmly towards refugees

00:45:21.790 --> 00:45:23.740
because they don't see them
as threatening anymore.

00:45:23.740 --> 00:45:26.530
But in fact, that doesn't happen at all.

00:45:26.530 --> 00:45:29.770
There's no broader
spillover of the information

00:45:30.880 --> 00:45:33.103
into these views of refugees.

00:45:34.040 --> 00:45:35.820
However, these empathy narratives,

00:45:35.820 --> 00:45:37.560
the perspective getting treatments

00:45:37.560 --> 00:45:41.970
do raise the average feeling
thermometer score quite a lot.

00:45:41.970 --> 00:45:45.080
So by an average of six percentage points

00:45:45.080 --> 00:45:48.660
on the scale from 62 to 68.5.

00:45:48.660 --> 00:45:50.810
And again, this is pretty consistent,

00:45:50.810 --> 00:45:54.170
whether it's perspective
getting on its own,

00:45:54.170 --> 00:45:55.950
or if that story about Abdi

00:45:55.950 --> 00:45:58.480
is combined with the information.

00:45:58.480 --> 00:46:01.030
And again, we don't see
any interaction here

00:46:01.030 --> 00:46:04.793
where combining the two makes
them even more effective.

00:46:07.260 --> 00:46:11.720
The third outcome has to
do with policy preferences.

00:46:11.720 --> 00:46:14.280
So we tell them every year
the federal government decides

00:46:14.280 --> 00:46:16.420
how many refugees to allow to be resettled

00:46:16.420 --> 00:46:17.890
in the United States.

00:46:17.890 --> 00:46:19.800
And we then give them some context

00:46:19.800 --> 00:46:21.677
so they know what those
numbers have looked like

00:46:21.677 --> 00:46:23.950
and how much they've bounced around.

00:46:23.950 --> 00:46:26.030
We say this year the number
of refugees permitted

00:46:26.030 --> 00:46:28.400
to enter is 125,000,

00:46:28.400 --> 00:46:31.280
next year do you think the
number should be higher, lower,

00:46:31.280 --> 00:46:32.653
or stay the same?

00:46:34.310 --> 00:46:35.143
And so again,

00:46:35.143 --> 00:46:38.630
information here providing
that correction on vetting

00:46:39.536 --> 00:46:43.460
doesn't make people more supportive

00:46:43.460 --> 00:46:46.530
of admitting more refugees, if anything,

00:46:46.530 --> 00:46:48.730
there's actually a slight backlash here,

00:46:48.730 --> 00:46:51.213
although that is not
statistically significant.

00:46:52.280 --> 00:46:55.930
However, again, giving
the narrative about Abdi

00:46:55.930 --> 00:46:58.260
does seem to make people more willing

00:46:58.260 --> 00:46:59.730
to say they would want to increase

00:46:59.730 --> 00:47:01.900
the number of refugees admitted

00:47:01.900 --> 00:47:04.990
that's significant and
substantively fairly meaningful.

00:47:04.990 --> 00:47:07.410
And again, combining the two

00:47:07.410 --> 00:47:11.200
doesn't really do much difference
as we thought it might.

00:47:11.200 --> 00:47:14.070
It's slightly higher for the combination,

00:47:14.070 --> 00:47:16.153
but that difference is not significant.

00:47:18.420 --> 00:47:19.700
And then finally we ask,

00:47:19.700 --> 00:47:21.040
would you be willing to write a letter

00:47:21.040 --> 00:47:23.330
to the current president's administration

00:47:23.330 --> 00:47:25.690
advocating for your position
on how many refugees

00:47:25.690 --> 00:47:28.160
should be admitted into the United States?

00:47:28.160 --> 00:47:29.120
So they could say yes,

00:47:29.120 --> 00:47:32.360
they would like to write a
letter supporting an increase.

00:47:32.360 --> 00:47:35.710
Yes, they would like to write
a letter opposing an increase,

00:47:35.710 --> 00:47:37.470
or no they would not
like to write a letter.

00:47:37.470 --> 00:47:41.140
And if they said yes, in
support or opposition,

00:47:41.140 --> 00:47:42.910
we asked them to actually write the letter

00:47:42.910 --> 00:47:45.270
to make this as behavioral as possible.

00:47:45.270 --> 00:47:47.950
And we said, and we
will send it anonymously

00:47:47.950 --> 00:47:50.260
to the administration on their behalf

00:47:50.260 --> 00:47:52.173
after completion of the survey.

00:47:53.970 --> 00:47:55.640
So this is what the
results look like here.

00:47:55.640 --> 00:47:58.210
And again, they're quite consistent where

00:47:58.210 --> 00:48:01.730
correcting on vetting, if
anything has a slight backlash,

00:48:01.730 --> 00:48:04.090
although again, this is not significant,

00:48:04.090 --> 00:48:06.750
so you're not seeing that spill over,

00:48:06.750 --> 00:48:09.420
however, the perspective
getting treatments on their own

00:48:09.420 --> 00:48:12.910
and in combination with the information do

00:48:12.910 --> 00:48:15.530
increase willingness to
write a supportive letter

00:48:15.530 --> 00:48:17.033
for raising the refugee cap.

00:48:20.120 --> 00:48:23.350
So to summarize these main
effects of the treatments,

00:48:23.350 --> 00:48:26.130
we do find that misconceptions
on security threat

00:48:26.130 --> 00:48:27.870
can be corrected,

00:48:27.870 --> 00:48:31.290
whether that's information just
on its own blandly provided,

00:48:31.290 --> 00:48:32.830
or whether that's embedded

00:48:32.830 --> 00:48:34.963
in a perspective getting exercise.

00:48:35.920 --> 00:48:38.210
However, that information on its own

00:48:38.210 --> 00:48:40.650
doesn't really seem to improve attitudes

00:48:40.650 --> 00:48:42.960
and behaviors relative towards the control

00:48:42.960 --> 00:48:44.770
and recall here that we were trying

00:48:44.770 --> 00:48:47.790
to pick a piece of information
where clearly people

00:48:47.790 --> 00:48:52.110
often had it wrong and where
also it seems theoretically

00:48:52.110 --> 00:48:54.700
and also from some of the
regression analysis we did

00:48:54.700 --> 00:48:58.920
that this should be related
to attitudes towards refugees.

00:48:58.920 --> 00:49:01.640
So we were trying to make
this as strong as possible,

00:49:01.640 --> 00:49:04.840
but still it didn't really work

00:49:04.840 --> 00:49:07.110
beyond just correcting the misconception.

00:49:07.110 --> 00:49:09.020
That being said, of course it may well be

00:49:09.020 --> 00:49:10.570
that another piece of information

00:49:10.570 --> 00:49:11.640
would have been more effective

00:49:11.640 --> 00:49:13.183
and maybe we just chose wrong.

00:49:14.510 --> 00:49:15.470
The other hand,

00:49:15.470 --> 00:49:18.410
we do find that perspective
getting on its own

00:49:18.410 --> 00:49:20.220
or with information

00:49:20.220 --> 00:49:22.420
really does improve
attitudes and behaviors

00:49:22.420 --> 00:49:24.403
at least in the context of the survey,

00:49:25.470 --> 00:49:27.920
making respondents more inclusive

00:49:27.920 --> 00:49:31.363
in how they approach refugees
as people and also policies.

00:49:32.270 --> 00:49:34.310
However, this doesn't actually cause them

00:49:34.310 --> 00:49:36.920
to update their beliefs
about the vetting process,

00:49:36.920 --> 00:49:39.423
which is not necessarily surprising.

00:49:41.410 --> 00:49:43.530
So now I wanna talk about

00:49:43.530 --> 00:49:46.150
the heterogeneous treatment
effects I had mentioned

00:49:47.060 --> 00:49:49.130
in terms of whether that perspective

00:49:49.130 --> 00:49:51.050
getting treatment specifically

00:49:51.050 --> 00:49:53.100
is more or less likely to be effective

00:49:53.100 --> 00:49:55.880
based on disposition empathy.

00:49:55.880 --> 00:49:58.080
And so again, it could be that

00:49:58.080 --> 00:50:00.260
those with more empathy actually

00:50:00.260 --> 00:50:02.400
respond less well to this treatment

00:50:02.400 --> 00:50:04.330
or that those with more empathy

00:50:04.330 --> 00:50:08.940
respond better because they're
more capable of processing

00:50:08.940 --> 00:50:10.563
the out-group's perspective.

00:50:12.230 --> 00:50:15.840
And in fact what we see
for the three outcomes

00:50:15.840 --> 00:50:18.610
where the perspective getting mattered

00:50:18.610 --> 00:50:22.230
was that these effects were
much stronger among individuals

00:50:22.230 --> 00:50:24.890
who had higher levels of baseline empathy.

00:50:24.890 --> 00:50:26.170
So in other words,

00:50:26.170 --> 00:50:29.803
people who are generally better
at empathizing with others,

00:50:31.530 --> 00:50:34.740
were more likely to increase
their inclusive views

00:50:34.740 --> 00:50:38.490
in response to the treatment
than individuals who

00:50:38.490 --> 00:50:41.480
just for whatever reason
are less empathetic.

00:50:41.480 --> 00:50:44.430
So this is what it looks like
with the feeling thermometer.

00:50:45.570 --> 00:50:48.440
Among individuals who are below average

00:50:48.440 --> 00:50:49.810
in their baseline empathy,

00:50:49.810 --> 00:50:54.460
the perspective getting
exercise with Abdi increased

00:50:54.460 --> 00:50:56.100
the average response on the thermometer

00:50:56.100 --> 00:50:58.380
by about four percentage points.

00:50:58.380 --> 00:51:01.450
However, among those with a higher level

00:51:01.450 --> 00:51:03.580
of baseline empathy,

00:51:03.580 --> 00:51:06.580
the average on the
thermometer almost doubled,

00:51:06.580 --> 00:51:08.360
the treatment effect almost doubled

00:51:08.360 --> 00:51:09.580
to eight percentage point.

00:51:09.580 --> 00:51:14.493
So this difference is
significant and fairly large.

00:51:16.360 --> 00:51:21.360
- Sorry, Scott are
these results sort of...

00:51:26.150 --> 00:51:30.900
Are they the same by gender
or by other characteristics of

00:51:33.760 --> 00:51:38.760
are they still obviously
find the same level of

00:51:38.778 --> 00:51:41.647
positive, or for example,
you would find that one is

00:51:42.647 --> 00:51:44.570
more than the other.

00:51:44.570 --> 00:51:48.430
- So I believe we've
looked with partisanship

00:51:48.430 --> 00:51:53.430
and it doesn't seem to
differ consistently.

00:51:55.890 --> 00:51:59.000
And likewise, I'm not sure about gender,

00:51:59.000 --> 00:52:02.020
but that would be a good
one for us to look at.

00:52:02.020 --> 00:52:05.620
There definitely may be other
heterogeneous effects here

00:52:05.620 --> 00:52:07.793
that we want to look at,

00:52:08.660 --> 00:52:12.690
but this pattern does hold
with controls included,

00:52:12.690 --> 00:52:17.690
it seems like as well as we can do,

00:52:18.640 --> 00:52:22.680
since we're not randomly
assigning levels of empathy,

00:52:22.680 --> 00:52:25.453
that this pattern does persist.

00:52:27.390 --> 00:52:30.850
And I guess for me,
partly it's quite strong

00:52:30.850 --> 00:52:33.160
because it's consistent
across those three outcomes.

00:52:33.160 --> 00:52:37.890
So this is what it looks
like for the policy outcome

00:52:37.890 --> 00:52:40.660
where you actually see a slightly

00:52:40.660 --> 00:52:43.580
negative effect for those low in empathy

00:52:43.580 --> 00:52:46.660
for the combined treatment
although not significant,

00:52:46.660 --> 00:52:48.830
but you see quite large effects

00:52:48.830 --> 00:52:52.523
and positive effects for
those high in empathy.

00:52:53.650 --> 00:52:55.863
And then again, also with letter writing,

00:52:56.940 --> 00:52:59.660
there's a slight boost for
those low and baseline empathy,

00:52:59.660 --> 00:53:03.320
but there's a fairly large boost

00:53:03.320 --> 00:53:05.570
for those high in baseline empathy.

00:53:05.570 --> 00:53:08.620
So, you know, when we're
doing this analysis,

00:53:08.620 --> 00:53:10.190
it may be that there's some

00:53:11.660 --> 00:53:15.053
other omitted variable explaining
the heterogeneous effects.

00:53:16.110 --> 00:53:19.240
We can't rule that out, but
it seems like on average,

00:53:19.240 --> 00:53:23.150
those with a higher capacity
to empathize with others

00:53:23.150 --> 00:53:27.600
in general are the ones
who are most likely to be

00:53:27.600 --> 00:53:30.690
affected by treatments
that try to prime empathy.

00:53:30.690 --> 00:53:34.040
And I do think logically that makes sense

00:53:35.120 --> 00:53:36.800
and isn't so surprising.

00:53:36.800 --> 00:53:39.720
So practically that might suggest that

00:53:39.720 --> 00:53:42.710
these are the kinds of
people to target with these

00:53:42.710 --> 00:53:45.203
exercises when you are
designing messaging.

00:53:47.150 --> 00:53:51.163
So just to start wrapping
up in terms of implications,

00:53:52.380 --> 00:53:56.630
there is some debate
over whether trying to

00:53:56.630 --> 00:53:59.640
reduce prejudice generally
or change attitudes on

00:53:59.640 --> 00:54:02.880
refugees and immigration is
better served by doing this

00:54:02.880 --> 00:54:05.260
sort of information correction

00:54:05.260 --> 00:54:09.060
or by trying to leverage
empathy about specific people

00:54:09.060 --> 00:54:10.960
and their stories.

00:54:10.960 --> 00:54:13.950
And our study is not the first to show,

00:54:13.950 --> 00:54:16.490
but builds on others suggesting that

00:54:16.490 --> 00:54:18.540
these empathy generating treatments

00:54:18.540 --> 00:54:23.470
are more effective typically
than trying to correct people's

00:54:23.470 --> 00:54:27.210
stereotypes or falsely held beliefs.

00:54:27.210 --> 00:54:30.690
But again, those effects
seem to be stronger for

00:54:30.690 --> 00:54:32.030
those with high baseline empathy.

00:54:32.030 --> 00:54:36.570
And in fact, they may not
exist at all for those who

00:54:36.570 --> 00:54:40.040
are lower on average and
in empathizing with others.

00:54:40.040 --> 00:54:40.940
At the same time,

00:54:40.940 --> 00:54:43.520
we do show that correcting
misconceptions is possible,

00:54:43.520 --> 00:54:46.840
so when we told people
that the U.S government

00:54:46.840 --> 00:54:48.620
does all this extensive vetting,

00:54:48.620 --> 00:54:52.040
there wasn't that much
resistance to them agreeing

00:54:52.040 --> 00:54:55.370
with that information and
updating their priors and saying,

00:54:55.370 --> 00:54:57.580
okay, yeah, I had it wrong,

00:54:57.580 --> 00:54:59.770
this is how much vetting
they actually go through.

00:54:59.770 --> 00:55:01.250
So to the extent that we care

00:55:01.250 --> 00:55:04.003
about correcting misinformation,

00:55:05.200 --> 00:55:06.990
even if we're not showing in this study

00:55:06.990 --> 00:55:09.090
that that has spillover effects

00:55:09.090 --> 00:55:11.070
in other ways that we might care about,

00:55:11.070 --> 00:55:14.040
it does still seem that it
is possible to get people

00:55:14.040 --> 00:55:16.330
to update on these sorts of issues,

00:55:16.330 --> 00:55:18.700
even when they're closely tied to

00:55:18.700 --> 00:55:21.253
pretty touchy political
topics and debates.

00:55:22.610 --> 00:55:25.970
And to us, the main takeaway here is that

00:55:25.970 --> 00:55:27.910
interventions combining perspective,

00:55:27.910 --> 00:55:29.620
getting or perspective taking

00:55:30.630 --> 00:55:33.340
along with information
correcting misconceptions

00:55:33.340 --> 00:55:37.730
may be the most effective at
addressing a range of outcomes

00:55:37.730 --> 00:55:39.940
because even though we
didn't find an interaction

00:55:39.940 --> 00:55:40.810
as we were expecting

00:55:40.810 --> 00:55:43.030
where these treatments
became more effective

00:55:43.030 --> 00:55:45.090
by combining them together,

00:55:45.090 --> 00:55:48.410
the combined treatment was the one that

00:55:48.410 --> 00:55:50.290
corrected people's misconceptions

00:55:50.290 --> 00:55:53.070
and also had these effects on prejudice

00:55:53.070 --> 00:55:55.050
through the perspective getting narrative.

00:55:55.050 --> 00:55:56.870
So this may have practical implications

00:55:56.870 --> 00:55:59.670
for messaging that's
emphasized by advocates.

00:55:59.670 --> 00:56:02.910
If you're going to provide
information and update,

00:56:02.910 --> 00:56:04.670
try to update people's priors,

00:56:04.670 --> 00:56:07.440
you might want to work in
these humanizing stories

00:56:07.440 --> 00:56:08.300
along with it

00:56:09.540 --> 00:56:11.860
if your goal ultimately
is to increase support

00:56:11.860 --> 00:56:13.630
for admitting refugees

00:56:13.630 --> 00:56:16.180
alongside correcting those misconceptions.

00:56:16.180 --> 00:56:19.890
Likewise, if you're going to humanize

00:56:19.890 --> 00:56:22.150
refugees as part of a
prejudice reduction strategy,

00:56:22.150 --> 00:56:25.940
then you might also try
and work in information,

00:56:25.940 --> 00:56:30.663
make the most of addressing
as many outcomes as possible.

00:56:31.950 --> 00:56:33.970
In terms of where we go next,

00:56:33.970 --> 00:56:38.640
so we would like to think more
about heterogeneous effects

00:56:38.640 --> 00:56:40.630
and do some exploratory analysis

00:56:40.630 --> 00:56:45.630
and thinking about who these
interventions work best for,

00:56:45.880 --> 00:56:48.670
we're only showing this
short term in the survey,

00:56:48.670 --> 00:56:51.550
so it may be that these effects
dissipate pretty quickly,

00:56:51.550 --> 00:56:53.490
in fact, they probably do,

00:56:53.490 --> 00:56:56.080
so we may try to look at persistence

00:56:56.080 --> 00:56:58.320
and then potentially we'd be interested

00:56:58.320 --> 00:57:00.750
in trying to scale this up into

00:57:00.750 --> 00:57:04.310
an experiment with actual
messaging in the field,

00:57:04.310 --> 00:57:06.320
so that's potentially another step also.

00:57:06.320 --> 00:57:10.820
So if there are comments or
suggestions along those lines,

00:57:10.820 --> 00:57:12.703
I'd be happy to hear them.

00:57:13.890 --> 00:57:16.730
So, yeah, I'll end there,
thank you very much.

00:57:16.730 --> 00:57:17.570
Comments are welcome.

00:57:17.570 --> 00:57:21.090
You can also email me directly here

00:57:21.090 --> 00:57:24.400
and for the team of co-authors we do have

00:57:24.400 --> 00:57:28.120
a nascent website that you're
welcome to look at as well

00:57:28.120 --> 00:57:29.210
at this link.

00:57:29.210 --> 00:57:30.043
Thank you.

00:57:31.140 --> 00:57:32.690
- Yeah, thanks.

00:57:32.690 --> 00:57:37.300
I'm trying to remember exactly
for your first experiment,

00:57:37.300 --> 00:57:41.380
the what exactly how
you gave the information

00:57:41.380 --> 00:57:43.410
concerning the misconceptions.

00:57:43.410 --> 00:57:47.843
And again, I'm thinking about
crime versus refugee vetting.

00:57:49.270 --> 00:57:53.410
What is the distribution
of those misconceptions?

00:57:53.410 --> 00:57:57.030
And so I'm wondering why
you took refugee vetting

00:57:57.030 --> 00:57:59.660
for your second experiment.

00:57:59.660 --> 00:58:03.400
And if there's something to learn from

00:58:03.400 --> 00:58:08.400
doing the experiment on a
misconception that is not,

00:58:12.800 --> 00:58:16.690
I don't know that is not
the average misconception,

00:58:16.690 --> 00:58:18.830
but where there is a lot of variety

00:58:18.830 --> 00:58:21.160
in what that misconception is.

00:58:21.160 --> 00:58:24.123
So getting at some of the
heterogeneous effects, probably.

00:58:25.430 --> 00:58:28.740
- Yeah, so for the first study,

00:58:28.740 --> 00:58:33.740
we simply asked people to
try and describe refugees

00:58:34.410 --> 00:58:35.990
in terms of these characteristics,

00:58:35.990 --> 00:58:37.980
sort of one by one is questions
throughout the survey,

00:58:37.980 --> 00:58:40.410
so we didn't randomize anything here.

00:58:40.410 --> 00:58:44.110
We weren't trying to incentivize
getting the answer right.

00:58:44.110 --> 00:58:48.120
We just asked them to,
you know, say, give us,

00:58:48.120 --> 00:58:49.290
please give us your best guess

00:58:49.290 --> 00:58:53.657
for what percent of women
and children are for,

00:58:53.657 --> 00:58:55.390
sorry, what percent of refugees
are women and children?

00:58:55.390 --> 00:58:58.620
So and we also, we told
people explicitly, like,

00:58:58.620 --> 00:59:01.260
if you don't know, just try
to give your best guess.

00:59:01.260 --> 00:59:04.310
So there we were just
interested in seeing generally

00:59:04.310 --> 00:59:05.700
what the misconceptions might be,

00:59:05.700 --> 00:59:07.594
and for many of these answers,

00:59:07.594 --> 00:59:09.510
there is a lot of variations, so,

00:59:09.510 --> 00:59:10.810
this for instance is the percent

00:59:10.810 --> 00:59:13.740
of terrorist activity
conducted by refugees.

00:59:13.740 --> 00:59:15.450
Someone is saying a hundred percent

00:59:15.450 --> 00:59:18.580
I like to believe that that is an effort,

00:59:18.580 --> 00:59:21.200
a political statement, but maybe not,

00:59:21.200 --> 00:59:26.200
but clearly there's a lot of
variation here that could be

00:59:26.240 --> 00:59:29.990
played around with and I
do think if we replicate

00:59:29.990 --> 00:59:31.250
a version of this study,

00:59:31.250 --> 00:59:35.010
it might make sense to
use this misconception,

00:59:35.010 --> 00:59:37.020
for instance, instead of the vetting one

00:59:37.020 --> 00:59:38.520
to see how this looks with

00:59:39.620 --> 00:59:41.180
a slightly different piece of information

00:59:41.180 --> 00:59:43.620
that in some ways maybe even more direct

00:59:43.620 --> 00:59:45.720
in terms of what people are worried about.

00:59:47.270 --> 00:59:49.890
So I do think it would be valuable

00:59:49.890 --> 00:59:53.970
to play around with different
types of corrections,

00:59:53.970 --> 00:59:58.010
because again, one weakness
of this literature generally

00:59:58.010 --> 01:00:00.190
is that there are a million different ways

01:00:00.190 --> 01:00:01.990
that you can provide information

01:00:01.990 --> 01:00:04.590
correcting different misconceptions.

01:00:04.590 --> 01:00:05.470
And it's hard, you know,

01:00:05.470 --> 01:00:07.770
we can think through theoretically
why certain information

01:00:07.770 --> 01:00:09.920
might be better than others,

01:00:09.920 --> 01:00:13.530
but it may just be that this was the wrong

01:00:13.530 --> 01:00:14.780
piece to update people on.

01:00:14.780 --> 01:00:18.623
So that is definitely a
consideration to keep in mind.

01:00:20.957 --> 01:00:23.200
- [Susan] Thank you.

01:00:23.200 --> 01:00:27.243
- Would you be trying or is
it part of the exercise maybe

01:00:31.660 --> 01:00:36.593
going forward and also to
identify the people that are

01:00:38.430 --> 01:00:42.240
more sort of have more empathy

01:00:42.240 --> 01:00:47.210
because obviously then you
can sort of increase whatever

01:00:47.210 --> 01:00:48.640
policy you're trying to do,

01:00:48.640 --> 01:00:52.130
but I would not know how to do that.

01:00:52.130 --> 01:00:54.720
I don't if you have thought about that.

01:00:54.720 --> 01:00:57.450
- Yeah, that's a great suggestion.

01:00:57.450 --> 01:00:59.540
We haven't, I guess thought about that,

01:00:59.540 --> 01:01:04.530
so directly we have
analyzed the correlates of

01:01:04.530 --> 01:01:06.783
higher or lower baseline empathy.

01:01:09.220 --> 01:01:11.000
I am not remembering those precisely

01:01:11.000 --> 01:01:12.190
so I don't want to say something

01:01:12.190 --> 01:01:13.610
that might end up being incorrect,

01:01:13.610 --> 01:01:16.330
but we have looked at that and
you can definitely say like

01:01:16.330 --> 01:01:19.783
so and so is more likely to
have a high level of empathy,

01:01:20.700 --> 01:01:24.420
but thinking through

01:01:26.720 --> 01:01:30.510
perhaps how to identify
people with more empathy

01:01:30.510 --> 01:01:32.704
in groups that aren't known for it

01:01:32.704 --> 01:01:35.460
might be fruitful for instance,

01:01:35.460 --> 01:01:38.103
so that's something that
seems we're thinking about.

01:01:39.220 --> 01:01:42.020
- And what about the
characteristics of the individual?

01:01:42.020 --> 01:01:45.440
Like, I think when I'm
trying to convince, oh, yeah,

01:01:45.440 --> 01:01:47.900
I don't like to be called
chairman of my department,

01:01:47.900 --> 01:01:51.560
and what would you think
if your daughter was,

01:01:51.560 --> 01:01:53.900
you know, heading an economics department

01:01:53.900 --> 01:01:55.990
and she were recalled chairman,

01:01:55.990 --> 01:02:00.690
and it's because I can appeal to

01:02:01.640 --> 01:02:05.650
that characteristic of him
being a father to a daughter.

01:02:05.650 --> 01:02:08.440
And so what can you do potentially

01:02:08.440 --> 01:02:12.480
in terms of learning how to
correct these misconceptions

01:02:12.480 --> 01:02:13.580
and appealing to them?

01:02:15.290 --> 01:02:20.290
- So one, in our previous work,
looking at family history,

01:02:20.470 --> 01:02:23.040
we essentially try to do that because

01:02:23.040 --> 01:02:26.193
basically all Americans
come from elsewhere,

01:02:27.420 --> 01:02:28.623
not all, but most,

01:02:29.930 --> 01:02:33.320
you can draw on that as
a sort of shared identity

01:02:33.320 --> 01:02:36.220
or experience at some
point in your family's past

01:02:36.220 --> 01:02:37.800
that people can relate to,

01:02:37.800 --> 01:02:41.530
and we show that this might
increase empathy towards

01:02:41.530 --> 01:02:43.810
immigrants coming in today.

01:02:43.810 --> 01:02:47.800
So I definitely agree
that being able to link

01:02:49.840 --> 01:02:51.350
your own experience to something

01:02:51.350 --> 01:02:53.130
they've gone through themselves,

01:02:53.130 --> 01:02:55.480
or at least tangentially
or related to perhaps

01:02:55.480 --> 01:02:58.970
because their daughter has
faced gender discrimination

01:02:58.970 --> 01:02:59.803
and they've seen it,

01:02:59.803 --> 01:03:02.590
even if they're a man
who can't go through it,

01:03:02.590 --> 01:03:05.370
then that will be perhaps more effective

01:03:06.381 --> 01:03:10.720
as an intervention on that type of person

01:03:10.720 --> 01:03:12.830
because they're just more able to relate.

01:03:12.830 --> 01:03:16.070
So that may be the case
even for people who

01:03:16.070 --> 01:03:18.800
don't have high levels
of baseline empathy,

01:03:18.800 --> 01:03:21.010
if you can still find a way to

01:03:21.010 --> 01:03:23.030
link the treatment to something

01:03:23.030 --> 01:03:25.130
they have gone through personally,

01:03:25.130 --> 01:03:27.860
they may be more able to
empathize than with the target

01:03:27.860 --> 01:03:30.400
because they have some
sense of what it is like

01:03:31.320 --> 01:03:32.313
to go through that.

01:03:33.320 --> 01:03:34.500
And so in our earlier study,

01:03:34.500 --> 01:03:36.920
we actually don't find
heterogeneous effects

01:03:36.920 --> 01:03:38.160
based on baseline empathy

01:03:38.160 --> 01:03:40.480
and I think that's in part because

01:03:40.480 --> 01:03:44.550
even those individuals have
this shared family history

01:03:44.550 --> 01:03:47.703
at some point migrating
to the United States.

01:03:50.210 --> 01:03:52.923
- They still say, but
my family stood in line.

01:03:55.990 --> 01:03:59.460
That's the response that they always say.

01:03:59.460 --> 01:04:02.530
We're not standing in line
today, but they did back then.

01:04:02.530 --> 01:04:03.523
My family did.

01:04:04.492 --> 01:04:08.167
Even though they didn't,
we know they didn't.

01:04:08.167 --> 01:04:10.780
- Yeah, definitely got
those responses as well.

01:04:10.780 --> 01:04:15.123
But on average it seemed to
have some positive effect.

01:04:16.980 --> 01:04:21.520
- I just have one comment,
so is that we're running

01:04:23.950 --> 01:04:28.327
a survey about the seminars that we have

01:04:31.475 --> 01:04:33.430
offered for this academic year,

01:04:33.430 --> 01:04:35.800
so if people could just please

01:04:37.400 --> 01:04:39.170
complete it and send it to us

01:04:39.170 --> 01:04:41.550
so then we can prepare for next year.

01:04:41.550 --> 01:04:46.550
And so thank you very much
to Professor Scott Williamson

01:04:46.618 --> 01:04:50.170
for this wonderful presentation and

01:04:51.470 --> 01:04:56.470
just sort of to broaden the
possibilities that we have

01:04:56.890 --> 01:05:01.890
in order to change the perspective
of people about refugees.

01:05:03.920 --> 01:05:06.130
So thank you very much, everyone.

01:05:06.130 --> 01:05:11.130
And this is our last seminar
of the academic year.

01:05:11.300 --> 01:05:14.230
Any question (indistinct)

01:05:15.149 --> 01:05:16.232
- Thank you.

